WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.539
Alice and Bob sit in separate rooms.

00:00:02.539 --> 00:00:05.894
Each receives one card, marked zero or
one.

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:08.491
They can agree on a plan beforehand.

00:00:08.491 --> 00:00:11.940
Once the cards arrive, each must answer
with one bit,

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.895
without seeing the other card.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:17.168
For three deals, their answers have to
match.

00:00:17.168 --> 00:00:20.618
If both cards show one, their answers
have to differ.

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:23.459
Suppose they always answer zero.

00:00:23.459 --> 00:00:25.566
Which deal costs them a point?

00:00:25.566 --> 00:00:26.896
Pause and pick it.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:30.558
One and one.

00:00:30.558 --> 00:00:34.601
They give matching answers just when the
referee needs different answers.

00:00:35.125 --> 00:00:38.095
What if Alice alone changes her answer on
a one?

00:00:38.095 --> 00:00:40.079
Watch the lost point move.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:42.537
She saves the last deal.

00:00:42.537 --> 00:00:46.098
Now she loses when her card is one and
Bob's is zero.

00:00:46.098 --> 00:00:48.398
Could a cleverer plan win all four?

00:00:49.375 --> 00:00:51.692
Put each possible answer on a pin.

00:00:51.692 --> 00:00:55.942
To win the first deal, Alice on zero must
equal Bob on zero.

00:00:56.375 --> 00:01:00.259
To win the next two, Bob on one must
match Alice on zero,

00:01:00.259 --> 00:01:03.112
and Alice on one must match Bob on zero.

00:01:03.625 --> 00:01:05.139
Follow the green path.

00:01:05.139 --> 00:01:07.381
All four answers must now match.

00:01:07.381 --> 00:01:10.046
The last deal asks the two ends to
differ.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:15.043
With independent fair cards, every deal
has a one in four chance.

00:01:15.043 --> 00:01:18.627
A local plan can win at most three
quarters on average.

00:01:18.627 --> 00:01:21.363
Mixing plans cannot improve that limit.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.545
A quantum pair offers a different move.

00:01:24.545 --> 00:01:26.749
Let each card choose the angle

00:01:26.749 --> 00:01:30.339
at which its holder measures one half of
an entangled pair.

00:01:30.875 --> 00:01:35.217
At these angles the ideal quantum win
rate is about eighty five percent.

00:01:35.217 --> 00:01:37.671
Each answer alone still looks random.

00:01:37.671 --> 00:01:40.568
The pattern appears when you compare the
two logs.
